# Federated Identity Chaining

In this lab, I configured LinkedIn as a social identity provider in Auth0 and tested a federated login flow.

## Screenshots

### LinkedIn Login Through Auth0

./linkedin-login-button.png

### LinkedIn User Token

./linkedin-jwt-token.png

## What I Did

- Created a LinkedIn Developer Application
- Enabled Sign In with LinkedIn using OpenID Connect
- Added LinkedIn as a social connection in Auth0
- Enabled the connection for my application
- Logged in through LinkedIn using OIDC Debugger
- Reviewed the token claims returned after authentication

## What I Learned

The application does not communicate directly with LinkedIn.

The trust chain looks like this:

Application → Auth0 → LinkedIn

The decoded token showed:

```json
"sub": "linkedin|IRtQ4LM_5M"
```

which identifies LinkedIn as the upstream identity provider.

The token issuer was:

```json
"iss": "https://dev-q531a5rk2ye1qpm7.us.auth0.com/"
```

which shows that Auth0 issued the token received by the application.

Even though I logged in with LinkedIn, the application only trusts Auth0 and never sees how LinkedIn authenticated me.

## Key Takeaways

- Federated identity works through a chain of trust.
- Applications trust Auth0.
- Auth0 trusts LinkedIn.
- The application only sees tokens issued by Auth0.
- The subject identifier (`sub`) is the reliable identity key.
- Account linking may be required when the same person uses multiple login methods.
